WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:03.640
- What were some of your
first jobs in New York?

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.907
- Well, I was a guard at the
Metropolitan Museum of Art.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:08.740
- Oh!
- Yes.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.680
When I was 21 years old,
right out of college,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.490
and I studied theater and I, I,

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.810
I got in a play and I thought that I could

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.150
work the midnight to eight
in the morning shift.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.360
You know what that shift is called?

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.553
The graveyard shift, right?

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.270
- Yes, I've had that shift in jobs. Yes.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:23.650
- You have?
- Yes.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.740
- So I thought I could do
it, 'cause I was very young.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.720
I thought I could go,
rehearse, do the play,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.780
do the midnight to eight in the mor-

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.880
Well, the third night of that,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.320
they found me asleep
on a 300 year old bed.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.100
- Oh no.
- Yeah.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.450
A work of art, like in
one of the period rooms.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.830
- Oh my God.
- Yes. And I was fired.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.260
- Oh. (laughing)
- Yes.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.150
That's what happens, Kelly,
when fall asleep on the art.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.260
Yes, I was. And, and,

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.100
but later, here's the lesson.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.210
Years later when I became a writer,

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.260
I needed to write a sample script.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.000
So I had a partner out here in Hollywood.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.000
What are we going to write about?

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.670
Well, there was a show called Roseanne

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:00.560
that was on at the time,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.770
the first iteration of Roseanne.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:06.260
And I thought, what if John
Goodman's character gets a

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.420
second job at the local museum,

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.170
And he falls asleep on a 300 year old bed?

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.160
And we wrote that, and we
started sending it around,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.200
and people were going,
"what an imagination!"

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.421
That's great.
- You're like, "yeah,

00:01:17.421 --> 00:01:18.630
I totally thought it up."

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.120
- We got hired. So my
most humiliating moment

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.410
of my life became my ticket to everything.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.860
- But that's smart. I call
that clever. That's using--

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.570
- But people, you can take terrible things

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.880
that happened in your
life and turn them into--

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.600
- Yeah! Songs. And yeah, that's what I do.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.100
- Is it? All of country
music is that, isn't it?

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.253
- Yeah, all of pop and country, yeah.

